-- THE DEFECT IT CLOSED, PROVED SEPARATELY FIRST.

Before the fix, run against the live database with no JWT claims, exactly as a
server action running as the service role:

  ERROR:  P0001: PROBE FOUND THE DEFECT (rolled back). An escrow was born and
  its audit row names actor_id = NULL. private.escrow_audit_insert reads
  auth.uid(), which is null for the service role, and every escrow write runs
  as the service role.

EVERY AGREEMENT EVER OPENED WAS AUDITED TO NOBODY. The 22 September work found
and fixed exactly this shape on the UPDATE trigger, by reading the columns the
doors write, and the INSERT trigger was left reading a session that is never
there. One trap, two triggers, and only one of them had been walked.

The fix is `escrows.opened_by`, written by every opening door and read by the
trigger as `coalesce(auth.uid(), new.opened_by)`. The probe now asserts not
only that the actor is non-null but that it is THE PROPOSER, and that
`actor_source` reads `row` rather than `session`, so a future change that
starts recording the payer by default would fail rather than look right.

-- THE REFUSALS, WHICH ARE THE POINT OF A MONEY DOOR.

  stranger proposing into somebody else's thread  not_a_party
  member proposing with somebody outside it      not_a_party
  purchase_balance                               purpose_not_open
  purchase_deposit                               purpose_not_open
  zero amount                                    bad_amount
  proposing to oneself                           same_party
  an example property                            demo_listing
  a second proposal in one thread                already_open
  the payee funding what the payee is owed        not_a_party
  a stranger funding it                           not_a_party
  an empty balance                                insufficient
  funding an already funded agreement             not_fundable
  withdrawing one that is funded                  already_funded

`demo_listing` is the one that would have shipped as an outage. Every one of
the seven conversations on this database is about an example listing, so the
first person to open the composer would have hit the demo-listing trigger's
raised exception and read "That could not be done just now". The door asks
first now; the trigger still refuses anything that gets past it.

-- AND THE MONEY.

One hold, under `rm-esc-<escrow uuid>-hold` derived inside the database from
the row rather than passed in, exactly one `wallet_entries` row for the
agreement, the payer's spendable balance at a delta of ZERO across a deposit
and a hold of the same amount, and `private.escrow_invariants_check()` still
reporting the float identity I-2 holding afterwards.
